<Axiom id="AX_DAG_AND_TICKET_BOUNDARIES">
      **Topological order, no cycles. Default: minimum tickets — one per coherent spec unit.** Product / library scope → one ticket per module-spec. Infrastructure / interface scope → one ticket per discrete capability.

      DAG edges derive from real file-level dependencies, Bootstrap → consumers, and cross-scope `depends-on`. No «logical layer» phases — Ports → Adapters → Integrations is an analysis aid in discovery, NOT a ticket-multiplication rule. A Port and its single confirmed-v1 Adapter co-edit and live in one ticket.

      **Split one ticket into N only when BOTH conditions hold:**
      - **(P) Parallel sub-process potential** — N phase-subagents dispatched simultaneously; Target Files disjoint across all phases of each ticket; no Deps edge among the N.
      - **(C) Context budget overflow** — combined cascade Rules + Spec References + Target Files exceed comfortable single-context for one phase-subagent's phase.

      **NOT a reason to split:** audit milestone granularity (audit runs once per GROUP — every ticket of the spec, together, per `AX_AUDIT_HOOK` — regardless of ticket size or count, never once per ticket); logical layering (Port + Adapter ship together); partial shippability (consumer can't pick a half-module); human time-units (hours / days — irrelevant for AI execution bounded by context window).

      Sequential split (A → B with hard Deps) without (C) = pure overhead: B's phase-subagent re-reads what A's already loaded; zero wall-clock gain.

      Cross-scope: scope B DONE before A's tasks consuming B's outputs. Bootstrap tickets DONE before non-bootstrap of the same scope.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_BOOTSTRAP_TICKET_DERIVATION">
      **Bootstrap Requirements rows with Owner=`this-scope-task` become the minimum coherent capability workstreams per `AX_DAG_AND_TICKET_BOUNDARIES`.** Derive only layers demanded by the selected requirements: runtime → package manager/dependencies → language/compiler → quality/test tooling → app platform. This is a platform-neutral taxonomy, not a mandatory five-ticket stack; adapter ids bind the concrete platform facts.
      Each emitted node carries its closed owner kind: infrastructure rows → `infrastructure-flat`;
      product/library `this-scope-task` rows → `scope-bootstrap`; product/library module work →
      `module`. No fourth owner exists; interface never owns a task.
      - Rows with Owner=`external-prereq-scope` → cross-scope dependency edge, not a ticket here.
      - Rows with Owner=`operator-action` → noted in tracker index as a blocking external prerequisite, not a ticket.
      - For each selected layer declare `Capability Adapter`, `Provides Capabilities`, and exact `Requires Capabilities`; every requirement reaches exactly one current/upstream provider through the phase/task DAG.
      - Every package mutation declares `Bootstrap Action: dependency-install`, exact `Provides Packages`, and Target Files containing the adapter manifest plus future/active lockfile. Consumers declare exact `Requires Packages`. Several layers may write the same manifest/lock only when the DAG strictly serializes them; no global single-owner rule exists.
      - Populate Verification from the Bootstrap Requirements Resolution command. One package probe proves one exact package; do not merge unrelated package probes.
      - A causally coupled dependency → config → command-smoke workstream is one DAG-connected ordered
        chain; coherent spec/context boundaries may split it across serialized tickets or phases. Never gate
        an earlier node on a later artifact. Project-wide wrappers belong to readiness/full audit.
        Role=`probe` belongs to the unique test phase owning its mapped test file; `Required-by` is
        traceability, not phase routing.
      - The authoring and feasibility tool calls own adapter lookup, rule-file evidence, provider uniqueness/order, package materialization, shared-writer serialization, and smoke ownership diagnostics. Apply their exact finding and rerun; do not reconstruct those mechanics in prose.
      - If Bootstrap Requirements is absent from the spec → abort with `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING` (the spec did not pass discovery STEP_7).
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_TASK_ID_UNIQUENESS">
      **Task-ID `<ACR>-<slug>` is unique, semantic, and immutable.** The slug names the feature in
      1-2 words, ≤ 8 characters — not a sequence number, not a paragraph.

      - `sdd-new` generates and validates the ID — grammar, slug length, project-wide uniqueness (duplicate
        or prefix-conflict against every existing Task-ID). Never hand-roll the format: use the exhaustive
        owner-specific `sdd-new task` ToolCall selected by STEP_3 and let it refuse with a concrete fix if
        the id is bad.
      - Uniqueness is enforced by the **Slug Registry** in each `<module>.3-tasks.md`: the same slug in two
        branches collides on merge there, surfacing «same feature» instead of hiding it. Append-only.
      - The ID never changes — even if the ticket file moves between scopes.
      - Code references via file headers (`@tasks:`) point to IDs, not paths.
      - `sdd-check` cross-checks integrity (SDD_TASK_ID_GRAMMAR, SDD_TASK_ID_PREFIX_CLASH,
        SDD_TASK_ID_COLLISION); a genuine collision → abort, operator resolves.
    </Axiom>

<Axiom id="AX_CROSS_SCOPE_TASK_PLACEMENT">
      Cross-scope integration tasks belong to the scope that **initiates or owns** the integration. If both sides have equal weight, create a dedicated `integration` scope in the Scope Graph and register it in `specs/README.md`. Never scatter pieces of one integration across multiple scope folders, and never invent a shared folder outside the Scope Graph for them.
    </Axiom>

